1
00:00:04,520 --> 00:00:05,080
Nine at night.

2
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:07,521
Your big exam is tomorrow morning.

3
00:00:08,101 --> 00:00:09,902
There are still three chapters you haven't

4
00:00:09,942 --> 00:00:10,422
even opened.

5
00:00:11,142 --> 00:00:11,923
So you make a plan.

6
00:00:12,743 --> 00:00:13,463
No sleep tonight.

7
00:00:14,043 --> 00:00:15,664
You pour a big mug of coffee and

8
00:00:15,704 --> 00:00:16,464
open the first book.

9
00:00:17,104 --> 00:00:17,465
Midnight.

10
00:00:17,965 --> 00:00:18,545
Two in the morning.

11
00:00:19,165 --> 00:00:20,886
The words start to swim on the page.

12
00:00:21,626 --> 00:00:23,487
At six, the sky turns gray.

13
00:00:24,047 --> 00:00:25,107
You've read everything once.

14
00:00:25,888 --> 00:00:27,768
At nine, you sit down in the exam

15
00:00:27,788 --> 00:00:28,929
room and turn over the paper.

16
00:00:29,649 --> 00:00:30,769
And the answer you read at three in

17
00:00:30,809 --> 00:00:32,550
the morning is simply gone.

18
00:00:33,310 --> 00:00:34,671
You feel foggy, grumpy,

19
00:00:34,711 --> 00:00:35,991
and strangely slow.

20
00:00:36,851 --> 00:00:38,452
Three days later, you come down with a

21
00:00:38,492 --> 00:00:38,812
cold.

22
00:00:39,592 --> 00:00:41,213
Was that all-nighter really worth it?

23
00:00:41,713 --> 00:00:42,573
Here's a strange fact.

24
00:00:42,974 --> 00:00:44,534
You spend about a third of your life

25
00:00:44,594 --> 00:00:44,955
asleep.

26
00:00:45,655 --> 00:00:47,396
Lying still, eyes closed,

27
00:00:47,816 --> 00:00:49,097
unable to react to danger.

28
00:00:49,917 --> 00:00:50,897
For an animal in the wild,

29
00:00:51,378 --> 00:00:52,898
that sounds like a terrible idea.

30
00:00:53,979 --> 00:00:56,580
And yet, scientists have never found an animal

31
00:00:56,620 --> 00:00:58,061
that clearly doesn't sleep.

32
00:00:58,801 --> 00:01:00,662
So sleep must be doing something important.

33
00:01:01,202 --> 00:01:02,223
But what, exactly?

34
00:01:02,783 --> 00:01:03,704
Let's collect some clues.

35
00:01:04,545 --> 00:01:05,045
Clue one.

36
00:01:05,786 --> 00:01:07,707
Put sensors on someone's head while they sleep,

37
00:01:08,148 --> 00:01:09,749
and you can watch their brain waves.

38
00:01:10,330 --> 00:01:12,512
In deep sleep, the waves become slow and

39
00:01:12,672 --> 00:01:15,454
huge, like a whole stadium chanting together.

40
00:01:16,235 --> 00:01:17,796
Later in the night comes REM sleep,

41
00:01:18,277 --> 00:01:19,718
short for rapid eye movement.

42
00:01:20,359 --> 00:01:22,200
That's when most vivid dreams happen.

43
00:01:22,681 --> 00:01:24,702
And during REM, the brain is about as

44
00:01:24,923 --> 00:01:26,004
active as when you're awake.

45
00:01:26,664 --> 00:01:28,526
So the brain doesn't simply switch off at

46
00:01:28,586 --> 00:01:28,766
night.

47
00:01:29,246 --> 00:01:29,686
Clue two.

48
00:01:30,227 --> 00:01:32,148
Learn something, then sleep on it,

49
00:01:32,729 --> 00:01:34,470
and you usually remember it better than if

50
00:01:34,510 --> 00:01:35,951
you'd stayed awake for the same number of

51
00:01:35,991 --> 00:01:36,292
hours.

52
00:01:37,533 --> 00:01:38,053
Clue three.

53
00:01:38,754 --> 00:01:40,535
In one experiment, people stayed awake all

54
00:01:40,575 --> 00:01:42,276
night, then tried to learn a set of

55
00:01:42,357 --> 00:01:43,257
pictures the next day.

56
00:01:44,098 --> 00:01:46,340
Two days later, they remembered about nineteen

57
00:01:46,420 --> 00:01:49,202
percent less than people who had slept normally.

58
00:01:49,702 --> 00:01:50,203
Clue four.

59
00:01:50,643 --> 00:01:52,885
Researchers measured how long people slept

60
00:01:52,945 --> 00:01:53,385
for a week.

61
00:01:54,066 --> 00:01:55,928
Then they dripped a cold virus into their

62
00:01:55,968 --> 00:01:58,850
noses, on purpose, and kept them in quarantine.

63
00:01:59,771 --> 00:02:01,693
People who had slept less than six hours

64
00:02:01,753 --> 00:02:04,175
a night were about four times more likely

65
00:02:04,215 --> 00:02:06,357
to catch the cold than those who slept

66
00:02:06,417 --> 00:02:07,078
more than seven.

67
00:02:07,598 --> 00:02:09,099
So what is sleep mainly for?

68
00:02:10,199 --> 00:02:12,601
A: your body powers down to save energy,

69
00:02:13,021 --> 00:02:14,302
like a phone on low battery.

70
00:02:15,262 --> 00:02:17,643
B: your brain switches off to rest,

71
00:02:17,963 --> 00:02:19,384
like a car parked in the garage.

72
00:02:20,305 --> 00:02:22,686
Or C: your brain and body start a

73
00:02:22,726 --> 00:02:24,507
night shift, filing memories,

74
00:02:24,847 --> 00:02:26,728
doing maintenance, and building defenses.

75
00:02:27,289 --> 00:02:28,930
Pause the video and take a guess.

76
00:02:37,728 --> 00:02:38,410
The key is this.

77
00:02:39,192 --> 00:02:40,454
Sleep isn't an off switch.

78
00:02:40,515 --> 00:02:41,657
It's a different work mode.

79
00:02:42,239 --> 00:02:43,141
To see what that work is,

80
00:02:43,602 --> 00:02:45,285
let's start with how you fall asleep in

81
00:02:45,305 --> 00:02:45,887
the first place.

82
00:02:46,447 --> 00:02:47,428
From the moment you wake up,

83
00:02:47,848 --> 00:02:50,489
a chemical called adenosine slowly builds

84
00:02:50,549 --> 00:02:51,150
up in your brain.

85
00:02:51,870 --> 00:02:52,831
The longer you stay awake,

86
00:02:52,871 --> 00:02:53,491
the more there is,

87
00:02:53,911 --> 00:02:55,212
and the sleepier you feel.

88
00:02:56,012 --> 00:02:57,713
Scientists call this sleep pressure.

89
00:02:58,394 --> 00:03:00,335
While you sleep, the adenosine is cleared

90
00:03:00,355 --> 00:03:02,076
away, and you wake up refreshed.

91
00:03:02,916 --> 00:03:05,118
Caffeine works by sitting in adenosine's parking

92
00:03:05,178 --> 00:03:05,958
spots in the brain.

93
00:03:06,739 --> 00:03:07,859
The adenosine is still there.

94
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:09,320
You just can't feel it.

95
00:03:09,540 --> 00:03:10,701
And caffeine lingers.

96
00:03:11,341 --> 00:03:12,982
For many adults, half of it is still

97
00:03:13,002 --> 00:03:14,563
in the body four to six hours later.

98
00:03:15,403 --> 00:03:17,625
In one study, a strong dose of caffeine

99
00:03:17,685 --> 00:03:20,366
taken six hours before bed cut sleep by

100
00:03:20,406 --> 00:03:20,947
about an hour.

101
00:03:22,127 --> 00:03:23,848
The second force is your body clock.

102
00:03:24,429 --> 00:03:25,250
Deep in your brain,

103
00:03:25,370 --> 00:03:27,471
a tiny master clock runs on a cycle

104
00:03:27,491 --> 00:03:29,473
of about twenty-four hours and eleven minutes.

105
00:03:30,334 --> 00:03:32,295
Every morning, daylight resets it to match

106
00:03:32,335 --> 00:03:32,736
the sun.

107
00:03:33,396 --> 00:03:34,737
In the evening, as it gets dark,

108
00:03:35,118 --> 00:03:36,759
your body releases melatonin,

109
00:03:37,339 --> 00:03:39,501
a hormone that signals that night has come.

110
00:03:40,282 --> 00:03:41,903
When high sleep pressure meets the clock's

111
00:03:41,963 --> 00:03:44,185
night signal, you fall asleep easily.

112
00:03:44,785 --> 00:03:46,567
Bright screens late at night can blur that

113
00:03:46,627 --> 00:03:46,887
signal.

114
00:03:47,427 --> 00:03:49,549
Once you're asleep, your brain doesn't stay

115
00:03:49,569 --> 00:03:50,249
in one state.

116
00:03:50,910 --> 00:03:52,551
It moves through cycles of roughly ninety

117
00:03:52,591 --> 00:03:54,112
minutes, four to six times a night.

118
00:03:54,833 --> 00:03:55,393
Early in the night,

119
00:03:55,413 --> 00:03:57,174
the cycles are full of deep sleep.

120
00:03:57,815 --> 00:04:00,357
Toward morning, REM sleep takes over and gets

121
00:04:00,437 --> 00:04:00,777
longer.

122
00:04:01,458 --> 00:04:02,678
So if you cut your night short by

123
00:04:02,738 --> 00:04:04,380
two hours, you don't lose a little of

124
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:04,800
everything.

125
00:04:05,320 --> 00:04:07,102
You lose a big chunk of your REM

126
00:04:07,142 --> 00:04:07,402
sleep.

127
00:04:07,922 --> 00:04:09,762
Now, the first job of the night shift:

128
00:04:10,843 --> 00:04:11,183
memory.

129
00:04:12,183 --> 00:04:14,303
During the day, new experiences land in a

130
00:04:14,443 --> 00:04:16,504
small brain area called the hippocampus,

131
00:04:17,124 --> 00:04:17,764
like an inbox.

132
00:04:18,824 --> 00:04:21,104
During deep sleep, the brain replays the day's

133
00:04:21,144 --> 00:04:23,105
memories and moves the important ones into

134
00:04:23,165 --> 00:04:24,105
long-term storage.

135
00:04:25,025 --> 00:04:26,826
That's why sleeping after learning helps you

136
00:04:26,866 --> 00:04:27,246
remember.

137
00:04:27,866 --> 00:04:29,826
Many researchers think it also clears room

138
00:04:29,846 --> 00:04:31,666
in the inbox, ready for tomorrow.

139
00:04:32,607 --> 00:04:34,247
Skip the night, and the inbox stays full.

140
00:04:35,067 --> 00:04:36,387
That's what happened in clue three.

141
00:04:37,187 --> 00:04:38,788
New memories had nowhere to go.

142
00:04:39,868 --> 00:04:41,168
REM sleep seems to help too,

143
00:04:41,688 --> 00:04:44,289
linking new memories with old ones and smoothing

144
00:04:44,329 --> 00:04:45,129
out emotional ones.

145
00:04:45,769 --> 00:04:47,409
The second job may be cleaning.

146
00:04:48,110 --> 00:04:50,110
Your brain cells produce waste as they work,

147
00:04:50,430 --> 00:04:52,511
including a protein called beta-amyloid.

148
00:04:53,071 --> 00:04:54,491
In a 2013 study in mice,

149
00:04:54,911 --> 00:04:57,092
the spaces between brain cells grew by about

150
00:04:57,312 --> 00:04:58,712
sixty percent during sleep.

151
00:04:59,472 --> 00:05:01,453
Fluid flowed through more easily and washed

152
00:05:01,513 --> 00:05:03,533
out more of that waste.

153
00:05:03,613 --> 00:05:06,074
In 2019, brain scans of sleeping people showed

154
00:05:06,194 --> 00:05:09,235
waves of fluid pulsing through the brain in

155
00:05:09,315 --> 00:05:11,335
step with deep sleep's slow waves.

156
00:05:12,035 --> 00:05:14,616
But a 2024 mouse study found the opposite

157
00:05:14,656 --> 00:05:17,517
result, so scientists are still arguing about

158
00:05:17,577 --> 00:05:18,737
how this cleaning works.

159
00:05:19,357 --> 00:05:21,339
The third job is defense.

160
00:05:21,979 --> 00:05:23,441
Remember the cold virus experiment?

161
00:05:24,201 --> 00:05:26,643
Short sleepers caught the cold far more often.

162
00:05:27,164 --> 00:05:28,645
Sleep also affects vaccines.

163
00:05:29,406 --> 00:05:30,306
In one small study,

164
00:05:30,747 --> 00:05:32,448
young men slept only four hours a night

165
00:05:32,488 --> 00:05:33,949
for several nights around the time of a

166
00:05:34,010 --> 00:05:34,510
flu shot.

167
00:05:35,311 --> 00:05:37,072
Ten days later, they had about half as

168
00:05:37,112 --> 00:05:39,234
many antibodies as men who had slept normally.

169
00:05:39,694 --> 00:05:41,174
What if you sleep just a bit less,

170
00:05:41,394 --> 00:05:41,794
every night?

171
00:05:42,495 --> 00:05:44,435
In one experiment, people spent just six hours

172
00:05:44,495 --> 00:05:45,855
in bed each night for two weeks.

173
00:05:46,616 --> 00:05:48,136
Their attention slips kept climbing,

174
00:05:48,236 --> 00:05:48,956
day after day.

175
00:05:49,636 --> 00:05:51,577
By the end, their performance was close to

176
00:05:51,617 --> 00:05:53,097
that of people kept awake for a whole

177
00:05:53,137 --> 00:05:53,337
night.

178
00:05:54,057 --> 00:05:55,517
But they only felt a little sleepy.

179
00:05:56,138 --> 00:05:57,818
They didn't realize how impaired they were.

180
00:05:58,618 --> 00:06:00,879
On the road, drivers who'd slept only four

181
00:06:00,899 --> 00:06:02,819
to five hours in the past day had

182
00:06:02,859 --> 00:06:05,360
about four times the crash risk of those

183
00:06:05,400 --> 00:06:06,820
who'd slept seven or more.

184
00:06:07,380 --> 00:06:08,861
Your body clock isn't set the same as

185
00:06:08,901 --> 00:06:09,541
everyone else's.

186
00:06:10,241 --> 00:06:11,882
Some people are natural early birds.

187
00:06:12,522 --> 00:06:13,603
Others are night owls.

188
00:06:14,323 --> 00:06:16,024
And during puberty, the body clock shifts

189
00:06:16,104 --> 00:06:17,965
later, by up to about two hours.

190
00:06:18,805 --> 00:06:20,546
Many teenagers find it hard to fall asleep

191
00:06:20,566 --> 00:06:21,567
before eleven at night.

192
00:06:22,067 --> 00:06:23,648
That's one reason the American Academy of

193
00:06:23,688 --> 00:06:25,849
Pediatrics recommends that middle and high

194
00:06:25,869 --> 00:06:27,870
schools start no earlier than eight thirty

195
00:06:27,890 --> 00:06:28,350
in the morning.

196
00:06:29,190 --> 00:06:30,571
So a teenager who can't wake up at

197
00:06:30,611 --> 00:06:32,512
seven isn't always lazy.

198
00:06:33,112 --> 00:06:35,033
Their body clock is still set to night.

199
00:06:35,633 --> 00:06:37,294
What about sleeping in on the weekend to

200
00:06:37,374 --> 00:06:38,374
pay back the debt?

201
00:06:39,335 --> 00:06:40,555
In one 2019 study,

202
00:06:40,936 --> 00:06:42,596
people got about five hours of sleep a

203
00:06:42,636 --> 00:06:43,417
night during the week.

204
00:06:44,017 --> 00:06:45,298
On the weekend, they could sleep as much

205
00:06:45,338 --> 00:06:45,858
as they wanted.

206
00:06:46,498 --> 00:06:48,099
But they only caught up about an hour

207
00:06:48,179 --> 00:06:48,619
in total.

208
00:06:49,060 --> 00:06:50,760
And when the short nights started again,

209
00:06:51,201 --> 00:06:53,141
their bodies handled sugar even worse.

210
00:06:54,222 --> 00:06:56,023
Sleep works best on a steady schedule,

211
00:06:56,583 --> 00:06:57,844
not as a loan you pay back on

212
00:06:57,924 --> 00:06:58,364
Saturday.

213
00:06:58,904 --> 00:06:59,946
So, back to the question.

214
00:07:00,447 --> 00:07:01,608
The answer is C.

215
00:07:02,330 --> 00:07:03,652
Sleep does save a bit of energy,

216
00:07:04,152 --> 00:07:06,055
but that can't explain the memory and immune

217
00:07:06,075 --> 00:07:06,436
effects.

218
00:07:06,957 --> 00:07:08,619
And the brain clearly doesn't switch off.

219
00:07:09,501 --> 00:07:11,444
Sleep is an active night shift for your

220
00:07:11,484 --> 00:07:12,165
brain and body.

221
00:07:12,705 --> 00:07:13,765
So how much do you need?

222
00:07:14,686 --> 00:07:17,046
Sleep experts recommend at least seven hours

223
00:07:17,086 --> 00:07:17,847
a night for adults.

224
00:07:18,387 --> 00:07:19,467
Yet in the United States,

225
00:07:19,507 --> 00:07:22,388
about one in three adults regularly gets less.

226
00:07:23,328 --> 00:07:24,089
A few habits help.

227
00:07:24,809 --> 00:07:26,089
Wake up at about the same time every

228
00:07:26,129 --> 00:07:27,049
day, even on weekends.

229
00:07:27,730 --> 00:07:28,830
Get daylight in the morning,

230
00:07:29,190 --> 00:07:30,390
and dim the lights at night.

231
00:07:31,131 --> 00:07:33,211
Keep caffeine to the morning and early afternoon.

232
00:07:34,152 --> 00:07:35,752
Keep your bedroom dark and cool.

233
00:07:36,492 --> 00:07:38,373
And don't use alcohol as a sleep aid.

234
00:07:39,033 --> 00:07:39,833
It may knock you out,

235
00:07:40,193 --> 00:07:41,574
but it breaks up your sleep later in

236
00:07:41,594 --> 00:07:41,874
the night.

237
00:07:42,374 --> 00:07:43,695
So next time there's a big exam,

238
00:07:44,216 --> 00:07:45,037
try a different plan.

239
00:07:45,437 --> 00:07:46,999
Study, then go to bed.

240
00:07:47,620 --> 00:07:49,662
Let the night shift file what you learned.

241
00:07:50,403 --> 00:07:52,245
The next morning, the answers are where you

242
00:07:52,285 --> 00:07:52,605
left them.
